### 2.2. Flow danych – przykład end-to-end (Daikin split)

```
1. POLLING (Backend scheduler)
   ├─ Co 3 min: GET /api.onecta.com/v1/devices?accessToken
   └─ Zwraca: temp (actual/target), mode, power, errorCode

2. PUBLIKACJA MQTT (NestJS → ThingsBoard)
   ├─ Topic: tenant/client_001/device/split_01/telemetry
   ├─ Payload: { temperature: 22.5, setpoint: 23, mode: "cool", errorCode: 0 }
   └─ Via ThingsBoard HTTP/MQTT API (REST publish)

3. INGESTION (ThingsBoard)
   ├─ MQTT message ingestion
   ├─ Save to telemetry (time-series storage)
   └─ Trigger rules engine

4. RULES ENGINE (ThingsBoard)
   ├─ Condition: temp > 30 for 10 min
   ├─ Action: Create alarm "High temperature"
   └─ Webhook: POST /api/backend/alerts (notify backend)

5. ALERT HANDLING (NestJS backend)
   ├─ Receive webhook → validate tenant
   ├─ Store alert (alert_id, severity, timestamp)
   ├─ OPTIONAL: Call Dynamics API → create Case
   └─ OPTIONAL: watsonx.ai → check if anomaly

6. UI (Frontend React)
   ├─ GET /api/clients/{id}/devices
   ├─ GET /api/alerts?limit=20
   ├─ WebSocket: live telemetry updates (optional: sub MQTT via WS bridge)
   └─ Render: dashboard + BIM viewer (xeokit) overlay status

7. HISTORY (Dataverse Customer Service)
   ├─ Case created (Alert → Case via Power Automate)
   ├─ Case status tracked
   └─ Display in frontend: "linked to Case XYZ"
```

### 2.3. Multi-tenancy

**Architektura:** Logical multi-tenancy (single DB, shared resources, tenant_id column everywhere)

- **Isolacja:** Filtrowanie na poziomie API (Query filters), middleware @Tenant() guard w NestJS.
- **ThingsBoard tenants:** Każdy klient = jeden tenant w TB (separate customer portal).
- **MQTT ACL:** EMQX kontroluje dostęp: user `tenant_001_gateway` ma dostęp tylko do `tenant/client_001/**`.
- **Dataverse:** Każdy klient ma własny Account/Contact; relatywnie odseparowane Cases.

